1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:06,500
Two envelopes. One holds twice as much money as the other. You
pick one. Should you switch?

2
00:00:06,800 --> 00:00:13,033
Here is an argument that makes switching sound irresistible.
Call the amount in your envelope A.

3
00:00:13,200 --> 00:00:19,833
The other envelope could contain twice A, or half A. Give each
possibility a fifty percent chance.

4
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:26,967
Then its expected value is half of twice A, plus half of half
A. That is one point two five A.

5
00:00:27,267 --> 00:00:35,133
A twenty-five percent bonus, just for switching? But now apply
the same argument to the envelope you switched to.

6
00:00:35,300 --> 00:00:42,833
It says switch back. Then switch again. You have created an
endless money machine without changing either envelope.

7
00:00:43,133 --> 00:00:48,967
Let us pin down the money. Suppose the envelopes contain ten
dollars and twenty dollars.

8
00:00:49,133 --> 00:00:54,400
If you picked ten, switching gains ten. If you picked twenty,
switching loses ten.

9
00:00:54,567 --> 00:01:04,500
With a random initial choice, those equally likely gains and
losses cancel. Either envelope starts with an expected value
of fifteen dollars.

10
00:01:04,800 --> 00:01:12,467
Notice that A was ten in the first case, but twenty in the
second. It was not one fixed number across both cases.

11
00:01:12,633 --> 00:01:22,067
If you instead fix A to an amount you have actually seen, you
must ask a different question: how likely are the two
possibilities given that amount?

12
00:01:22,367 --> 00:01:30,633
That depends on how the money was put into the envelopes. Two
possible amounts do not automatically mean equal
probabilities.

13
00:01:30,800 --> 00:01:40,967
For a concrete example, suppose the organizer chooses one of
two pairs with equal probability: ten and twenty dollars, or
twenty and forty.

14
00:01:41,267 --> 00:01:45,733
You pick an envelope at random, open it, and find twenty
dollars.

15
00:01:45,900 --> 00:01:54,500
Here, the other amount really is equally likely to be ten or
forty. Switching has an expected payout of twenty-five
dollars.

16
00:01:54,667 --> 00:02:00,467
So the original calculation can be right for a particular
amount, under a particular model.

17
00:02:00,767 --> 00:02:08,767
But what if you open your envelope and find forty dollars? In
this same model, the other envelope must contain twenty.

18
00:02:08,933 --> 00:02:15,400
Eighty dollars is not possible. The fifty-fifty assumption has
vanished, and switching would lose money.

19
00:02:15,700 --> 00:02:23,633
Before opening, either randomly selected envelope has the same
expected value: twenty-two dollars and fifty cents.

20
00:02:23,800 --> 00:02:31,667
After opening, the amount you see can give you useful
information. That does not make every possible switch an
improvement.

21
00:02:31,967 --> 00:02:41,633
The glitch was not in multiplication. It was in treating an
unknown amount and a fixed observed amount as if they came
with the same probabilities.

22
00:02:41,800 --> 00:02:48,800
Before trusting an expected value, ask: what is random, what
is fixed, and what information am I using?

23
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:50,500
Smarter choices.

24
00:02:50,800 --> 00:02:52,233
A better future.

25
00:02:52,533 --> 00:02:53,967
Follow for more.
